# Tickets and branches

## Every merge to main is backed by a ticket (Never)

Nothing merges to `main` unless a ticket backs it: usually one issue in the project's tracker, occasionally several when the work genuinely spans more than one.
File the ticket first, then work against it, for process, tooling and docs changes as much as features and fixes.
This adds no gate beyond the existing ones; it only requires the ticket to exist before the PR merges, so the PR's `Ticket:` and `Requirements:` lines always have an issue to point at.

Every merge is also traceable to an owner request (SDLC-014): an evidence-based chain, each link completing "this change is necessary to complete …", ending at something the owner actually asked for.
That end is a ticket the owner raised and marked ready, an owner direction to start or un-park work, or a standing owner policy such as fixing Must and Should review findings (SDLC-037).
A ticket alone is not a request.
A ticket records the request it serves on a `Request-trace:` line only when both hold: the request isn't already evident from the ticket's structure (a parent or blocking link, a label the owner set directly, a review pass's stated provenance), and a real request exists to record (a parent ticket, a link to the owner's direction, or the standing policy's statement ID).
Never write a placeholder or best guess: when the request is not evident and no real one exists to record, the ticket has no owner request yet, and under SDLC-014 it doesn't queue or start until one does.
A ticket filed before this rule predates the field; some had an approved request never written down, so they are checked once, as a backfill audit, not treated as licence for a new ticket to go without one.
Design docs, ADRs and other docs need no citation of their own; their merge commit, PR and ticket carry the chain.

A closing step the ticket names (a design-doc status line, a tracker checklist tick, a cross-reference) is part of the same PR, not a follow-up.
The one exception is a closing step that depends on post-merge state, such as the feature pipeline's terminal-slice tick, checked after the merge and written by a small follow-up PR, never a direct write to `main` (`.agents/skills/implement-with-merge/SKILL.md`, steps 0 and 3).

## A scope check-in before generalizing an instruction (Never)

Before a skill or instruction change turns one of the owner's specific, one-off instructions into standing wording available to future tickets, the thread asks the owner directly "do you want this process change folded into the repository's instruction set?" (or words to that effect) and waits for a plain yes or no.
This confirms scope, not exact wording, and is additional to, not a substitute for, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge" (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`).
SDLC-014 states it directly: generalizing a specific instance into a standing rule or capability goes beyond what the request supports, unless the request asked for that generalization, and needs its own request before it merges.
A `Request-trace:` line can quote a genuine owner request and still miss this, because the request was for one instance, not the standing rule written from it.
A thread unsure whether it applies an already-standing rule or generalizes an instance into a new one treats it as the latter and asks, the same instinct as triage's "on any doubt, route to `ready-for-structure`" (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`).
The owner's answer is recorded as a comment on the ticket, in the owner's own words (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`'s evidence table).

## A ticket request is capture only (Never)

When the owner asks for a ticket of any kind, everything agreed goes into it: the problem, the owner's own words, decisions, and any drafted wording, including approved requirement wording.
Nothing is committed, pushed or opened as a PR for it until the owner confirms the work should start.
Approving wording during capture approves the wording, not the change; the ticket quotes that approval so the later spec-only PR can cite it.
An explicit instruction to start ("go", "do it now", "open the PR") is the confirmation, and a ticket the coordinator hands out under normal queueing counts as started.
If it is unclear whether the owner wants a ticket or the change itself, ask.

## Ask which state a requested ticket goes into (Never)

When the owner explicitly asks for a ticket without saying which state (now, `hanging` or `future`), ask before creating it; never create it first and correct it later, or infer the state from the wording.
This applies only where the owner asks for the ticket; tickets an agentic process files (review findings, sweeps, pipeline stages) take their state from that process.
`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Parking labels", defines the states; `hanging` items sit under the hanging epic {{ hanging_ref }} and `future` items under the deferred-ideas epic {{ ideas_ref }}.

## Choosing a path, and the soft path sanity check on entry (Default)

A ticket takes one of two paths.
The **direct path** is triage straight to `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure`, with no grilling and no pipeline.
The **feature pipeline** is `needs-grilling`, then `ready-for-feature-design`, then stages 3 to 8 (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`).

**Choosing the path**: the owner's words choose it (`instructing-agents.md`'s phrases for each path).
If they don't, the agent picks one and records `Path: direct|pipeline — <one-line reason>` in the ticket body.
The owner can override with one of those phrases at any time; this is not a new question to the owner.

**Soft sanity check on entry to each path**: **not** an up-front routing criterion; staying on the current path is the default, and the check only *may* propose a move.

- **Entering the direct path** (triage's outcome step, or the coordinator accepting a direct request): if the ticket will obviously change multiple source files, or solves a use case with multiple steps or constraints, propose the feature pipeline.
- **Entering the pipeline** (`.agents/skills/feature-pipeline/SKILL.md` job (a), before stage 2's interview): if a fairly localised change set with straightforward constraints can obviously meet it, propose the direct path.
- A move happens **only if the owner agrees**. Ask once per ticket, as a decision card with a recommendation, and record the answer on the ticket (`Path check: <stayed|moved> <path> — owner, <date>`) so it isn't asked again. With no answer, the ticket stays on its current path.
- **Exempt**, as already the output of a path: a slice ticket `to-tickets` files at stage 6, a findings ticket from a review or architecture pass, and a Main Health ticket.

## Branch names

Name a branch after what the change does, never the chat or session that produced it, which tells a reviewer nothing and stops being findable once the session ends.
Good names look like `fix-invoice-rounding` or `add-export-retry`; `claude/setup-skills-x7k2qp` is the shape the pre-commit branch check rejects.

An agent that cannot safely delete a branch after its PR merges may leave it for the owner; a skipped delete is not a failure, so do not retry it or report it as one.

## One branch per issue, taken fresh off main

When one session addresses a series of separate issues, each starts on its own fresh branch cut from the current head of `main`, never layered onto the previous issue's branch or working tree, and never off another in-progress feature branch.
Before each new branch, run `git fetch origin main` and branch from `origin/main` (`git checkout -b <branch-name> origin/main`), not a local `main` that fetch alone does not move.

Use the non-resetting `checkout -b`, never `-B`, which force-resets an existing branch of that name and silently discards its commits.
If the name already exists, let `-b` fail, then look at that branch before deciding whether to delete or reuse it.

Check `git status --short` is empty before checking out the new branch, committing or stashing (`git stash push -u`) anything it shows: `checkout -b` carries non-conflicting dirty changes into the new branch, so leftover edits silently ride into the next issue's diff.

The only exceptions are a small follow-up fix to a PR still open from the same session, or the owner explicitly asking for work to land on another in-progress branch.

Each ticket is built in its own `git worktree` by default, so tickets never share a `HEAD` or an index (`.agents/skills/implement-with-merge/SKILL.md`, step 1).

## Closing keywords

A PR that fully implements an issue says `Closes #<n>`; wording like "Implements" leaves it open.
Verify the issue's state after merge.

GitHub's closing-keyword scanner ignores quoting and prose: close, closes, closed, fix, fixes, fixed, resolve, resolves or resolved directly before an issue number, in the PR body or any commit message landing on `main`, closes that issue, and editing the PR afterwards fixes nothing.
It reads no leading "not", quoted example or markdown bold as negation: a PR body saying "This PR does **not** close #<n>" still closes #<n> on merge.
So break the adjacency whenever a standing tracker's number, or any issue a PR body or commit message explicitly says the change does not close, sits near one of those words, including in a quote of another PR's or commit's text and in a reproduced bad example, never reproducing a live keyword-adjacent reference verbatim (use the `#<n>` placeholder style above).
The standing trackers are the post-merge review tracker {{ pmr_ref }}, the deferred-ideas epic {{ ideas_ref }}, the hanging epic {{ hanging_ref }}, the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }} and the local full-suite evidence tracker {{ lfs_ref }}.

## Close epics the moment their sub-issues do

An epic has no closing PR, so when a sub-issue closure brings an epic to 100% complete, close the epic in that same pass.
Never close one of the five standing trackers above this way, whether sub-issues or run comments accumulate on it.
A feature's own tracker closes only after the extra checks in `.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, "Closing the tracker".
